00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.039 align:start position:0%
 
[Music]  it's an age-old saying good writers  borrow great writers steal you can  simply iterate on existing ideas or you  can study them understand them and make  them your own  of course the sentiment doesn't just  apply to writing it can be said about  any creative endeavor  including video games and in this regard  Call of Duty Black Ops for the latest in  the long-running yearly series has  stolen a lot and it is all the better  for it  leaving aside trite quotes black ops 4  is just a lot of fun in fact it's the  most fun I've had with any Call of Duty  since modern warfare 2 this caught me  off guard as an annual release Call of  Duty has felt stale for a while now  despite its trips back to World War 2  the dystopian future and the fringes of  outer space throw in the fact that  developer Treyarch didn't include a  single-player campaign this year and I  was all but turned off from the property  black ops 4 would be another entry in  the yearly ritual I had grown so tired  of I would log on play a few hours get  my fill and move on to the next triple-a  release or so I thought almost  immediately black ops 4 had me hooked  after four hours of multiplayer I  sincerely wanted to play more I wanted  to learn each specialist character I  wanted to find my main so to speak call  of duty has been inching closer to  becoming an actual hero shooter for  years now but in 2018 it's finally taken  the dive it's still very much Call of  Duty with a quick time to kill three  lane maps and an abundance of  progression options with which to outfit  your characters but it's also taken some  of the best parts of its hero shooter  contemporaries and made them its own  first and foremost are the heroes  themselves or in this case the  specialists in addition to the normal  creative class loadouts inherent to the  series each specialist has his or her  own unique gadget and weapon the gadgets  have a shorter cooldown and benefit you  in a variety of specialized scenarios  Ajax is nine bang for instance is great  for stunning enemies on a control point  serifs tactical deploy beacon on the  other hand gives your teammates an  alternate spawn point shortening the  distance to crucial objectives  specialist weapons function more like  ultimate abilities ruins grab slam can  clear an entire  of opponents while torques barricade  emits microwaves in a straight path in  recent iterations of the yearly  franchise character specific abilities  were a pretty minor the specialist in  black ops 3 had unique weapons and the  combat rigs of infinite warfare provided  additional perks but black ops 4 finds  ways to make your character selection  matter and to change how you play the  game the specialist recon who happens to  be my favorite relies on Intel and  positioning the character Nomad focuses  on traps and defensive skills if you're  not playing to each of their strengths  you might not be helping your team to  the full extent some of the most  successful multiplayer shooters today  make use of heroes overwatch and Rainbow  six siege are the first that come to  mind placing their rosters at the center  of their design  overwatch a superpowers feed into its  colorful bombastic gameplay while sieges  operator gadgets add even more  complexity to its high stakes rules the  thing that strikes me most about black  ops 4 is multiplayer is that I'm  constantly thinking throughout each  match Call of Duty games don't usually  encourage this the shooters have always  been aggressively decent allowing me to  go through the motions trading kills and  earning new weapons at a steady pace  black ops 4 however puts you at a  disadvantage if you don't make use of  your unique abilities on the flip side  more often than not it makes me feel as  if I'm actually earning my wins this  sentiment amplifies when you switch  between game modes defensive specialists  are crucial in defending control points  while Intel focused characters excel in  more punishing hardcore modes black ops  4 has borrowed something else from  overwatch and Rainbow six siege it's  storytelling focuses on backstory and  context allowing you to fill in the gaps  as you play each specialist has an intro  video along with dossiers that outline  their origins and career experience the  specialist HQ missions provide enough  story to make its characters memorable  without subtracting from the gameplay in  the process this focus on player driven  storytelling that is allowing players to  use context in the game systems  themselves to find their own stories has  become prevalent in recent years  overwatch has thrived on this kind of  narrative with tangential short videos  that add flavor to each character siege  even goes so far as to factor its  relationships into  it's gameplay the character Ecco is  immune to the Korean operator dhoka bees  hacking attack because they grew close  outside of combat and helped each other  improve their gadgets black ops 4  doesn't take things this far but I  wouldn't be surprised to see Treyarch  continually lean into its cast as the  game continues to grow it already has a  healthy roster of ten specialists and  I'm having a blast learning each one's  unique abilities time will tell if  there's longevity in this formula but  Treyarch already has a solid foundation  to build on of course for all of the  improvements that the  player-versus-player arena makes this is  only one third of the black ops 4  package and while the new zombies maps  are a joy and offer dozens of hours of  frantic cooperative play I want to turn  your attention to black out it's the  next in a growing number of battle  royale modes and one of the biggest  attractions for this year's Call of Duty  entry unless you avoided video games  social media or pop culture in general  for the past three years or so you're  probably familiar with the battle royale  formula 100 players drop onto a map  gather supplies and fight until they're  the last team or individual alive  blackout is just as straightforward as  you'd imagine it combines the structure  of a battle royale with the mechanics of  Call of Duty and it works it works  really well the tight controls and  comfortable aiming feel great on  blackouts map and a slightly longer time  to kill ensures that many will have a  fighting chance it also incorporates  zombies in a clever way certain areas of  the map are infested with a living dead  but also offer better loot for those  that brave the monsters it's a simple  risk reward system that adds character  to the map brings a unique wrinkle to  the battle royale formula and ties into  the overarching Call of Duty lore as was  the case with its PvP modes black ops 4  understands not just how to borrow from  other games but how to steal and in the  process make those elements even better  I have to hand it to Treyarch the team  clearly understands what makes Call of  Duty tick it could have implemented some  building mechanics a la fortnight or  gone completely overboard with the  number of zombies on its blackout map  but it held back black I was a simple  mode that works so well because it  simple it uses call of duty strengths to  make a seemingly obvious mode perform  extremely well it's ironic that Call of  Duty has expressed confidence in its own  identity  specifically by understanding the  identities of other games it recognizes  the value of the Battle Royale genre and  the practicality of hero shooters  storytelling and morphs them both into  something with their own distinct flavor  the result is a shot in the arm for one  of the most pervasive series in modern  games and that is no small  accomplishment  [Music]

